WN.lb(45,{"mt_G1-8_1yC6w":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which part smells a flower?","choices":["Nose","Mouth","Ears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sniff! Which part do you sniff with?","explain":"Your nose smells. Your mouth tastes and your ears hear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word tells how a drum sounds?","choices":["Loud","Fluffy","Sour"],"answer":0,"hint":"A drum is heard. Pick a word for ears.","explain":"Loud is a word for sounds. Fluffy is a feel word and sour is a taste word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You find a mushroom in the park. What do you do?","choices":["Just look, do not taste","Taste a little bit","Put it in your pocket to eat later"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a park mushroom food from a grown-up?","explain":"Mushrooms outside can make you very ill. Look, but never taste."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A lemon tastes...","choices":["Sour","Loud","Bumpy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word is a taste word?","explain":"Sour is a taste word, and a lemon is sour. Loud is for ears and bumpy is for hands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You hear {n} claps. Which part heard them?","choices":["Ears","Eyes","Mouth"],"answer":0,"hint":"Claps are sounds. Which part hears?","explain":"Ears hear sounds, so your ears heard the {n} claps.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which part tells you a pine cone is prickly?","choices":["Hands","Ears","Nose"],"answer":0,"hint":"Prickly is a feel word. Which part feels?","explain":"Your hands feel that the pine cone is prickly. Your nose smells it and your ears hear it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {n} cakes. Which part tastes them?","choices":["Mouth","Ears","Eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part do you eat with?","explain":"Your mouth and tongue taste, so your mouth tastes the {n} cakes.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is soap for tasting?","choices":["No, it is for washing","Yes, it smells nice","Yes, a little bit"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you do with soap at the sink?","explain":"Soap is for washing. It may smell nice, but it is not food, so do not taste it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of you hears a bell ring?","choices":["Ears","Eyes","Nose"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bell makes a sound. Which part takes in sounds?","explain":"Ears hear sounds, so your ears hear the bell. Eyes see it, but they can not hear it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You see {n} red balloons. Which part saw them?","choices":["Eyes","Ears","Hands"],"answer":0,"hint":"Seeing colours is a job for one part. Which one?","explain":"Eyes see colours, so your eyes saw the {n} red balloons.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A teddy is in a bag. You can not see it. How can you find out it is soft?","choices":["Feel it with your hand","You can not find out","Taste it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part can tell soft from hard?","explain":"Your hand can feel that it is soft, even with no looking. Never taste toys."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Gran gives you {n} grapes for snack. Is it OK to taste them?","choices":["Yes, food from a grown-up","No, never taste anything","Only if they are on the floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who gave you the grapes, and are grapes food?","explain":"Food a grown-up gives you is safe to taste, so you can eat the {n} grapes.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You have five helpers on your body. Eyes see. Ears hear. Your nose smells. Your mouth tastes. Your hands feel.","Shut your eyes. Can you still find out? Yes! Your hands can feel a soft teddy. Your ears can hear a bell.","Say what you find. A drum is loud. A lamb is soft. A sock can be smelly. A pine cone is bumpy.","Look at a thing, feel it and sniff it. But do not taste it. Only taste food from a grown-up."],"adult":["Young children learn about the world through their senses. At two and three, the goal is to use looking, listening, touching, smelling and, when safe, tasting to explore everyday things, and to name the body part behind each sense: eyes, ears, nose, mouth (tongue) and hands (skin).","The lab gives each idea its own action. Children send a rainbow, bell, flower, strawberry and teddy to the body part that senses them, find toys in a feely bag by touch alone, choose sense words such as loud, sour, soft and smelly, sort food from things that are only for looking, and explore a pine cone with three senses while the mouth stays shut.","Common mistakes: mixing up which part does what (smelling with the mouth, hearing with the eyes); thinking you can only find out by looking; using a word from the wrong sense, such as calling a drum fluffy; and putting non-food things in the mouth. Touch is felt by skin all over the body; hands are just the part children use most.","Keep the safety rule simple and firm: we taste only food a grown-up gives us. Berries, mushrooms and plants outside, soap, pills and small objects are for looking, not tasting."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, collect one safe natural thing, such as a pine cone, leaf or stone. Ask, \"What can your eyes tell you? Your hands? Your nose?\" Give one new word each time: bumpy, smooth, crunchy, smelly.","keyIdea":"You have five senses. Eyes see, ears hear, your nose smells, your mouth tastes and your hands feel. Use them to find out about things. Only taste food that a grown-up gives you.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sense does each word belong to? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👂 Hear","✋ Feel","👃 Smell"],"items":[["loud",0],["quiet",0],["squeaky",0],["soft",1],["bumpy",1],["prickly",1],["smelly",2],["stinky",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsense","caption":"Flagship lab: match things to body parts, find toys by touch, pick sense words, sort food from look-only things and explore a pine cone.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_a6UA5XD1SU":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which changes faster in the sun?","choices":["An ice cube","A banana","A rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which one you can watch change before lunch.","explain":"An ice cube melts fast, in about an hour in the sun. A banana takes days and a rock does not change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Before, the flower was shut. Now it is open. Is it the same flower?","choices":["Yes, it changed","No, a new flower","It did not change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did someone swap it, or did the same flower open up?","explain":"Yes, it is the same flower. It changed by opening up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ava looks at a leaf and a teddy at {n} o’clock and again at bedtime. The teddy is the same. Is that OK to say?","choices":["Yes, some things stay the same","No, everything changes","No, teddies melt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Noticing what stays the same is part of looking closely.","explain":"Yes. Good scientists say what changed and what stayed the same, like Ava did from {n} o’clock to bedtime.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Put these in order: white apple slice, a bit brown, very brown. Which is first?","choices":["The white slice","The very brown slice","The bit brown slice"],"answer":0,"hint":"The slice gets browner the longer it is left out.","explain":"The white slice is first. Then it goes a bit brown, and now it is very brown."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How can you see small things on a bug?","choices":["Look up close with a magnifier","Look fast from far away","Close your eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way makes tiny details big?","explain":"Look up close with a magnifier. A quick look from far away misses small details."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam has {n} ice cubes in a bowl. After a while they are gone and the bowl has water. Where did the ice go?","choices":["It melted into water","Into the sky","Sam threw it out"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is in the bowl now that was not there before?","explain":"It melted. All {n} ice cubes turned into the water in the bowl.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which words tell about a change?","choices":["Before and now","Red and blue","Up and down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words talk about time?","explain":"Before and now, or first, then and now, help you say what changed over time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A puddle is big in the morning and gone at night. What happened?","choices":["It dried up","It grew","Nothing changed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare morning and night. What is different?","explain":"It dried up. The puddle got smaller and smaller until it was gone."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ice sits in a warm cup. Later the cup has water and no ice. What happened?","choices":["The ice melted","The ice went away","The cup made water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where did the water in the cup come from?","explain":"The ice melted. It turned into the water in the cup, so it did not go away."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia checks a banana for {n} days. First yellow, then spots. What comes next?","choices":["Brown all over","Green again","Yellow again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bananas get darker as they get older, not lighter.","explain":"Next it goes brown all over. After {n} days or so, a banana keeps getting browner; it does not go back.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which tool helps you see a tiny seed in an apple?","choices":["A magnifier","A cup","A spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which tool makes small things look big?","explain":"A magnifier, also called a hand lens, makes tiny things look big so you can see the detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo looks at a puddle at {n} o’clock and again after lunch. It is smaller. What can he say?","choices":["Before it was big, now it is small","It is the same","It got bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use before and now to say what is different.","explain":"Before it was big, now it is small. Saying before and now describes the change Leo saw since {n} o’clock.","params":{"n":[8,11]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Put ice in a cup. Wait. Look again! The ice is water now. Before it was hard. Now it is wet.","Look at a banana each day. First it is yellow. Then it gets spots. Now it is brown.","A magnifier makes small things look big. Look up close. You may see a tiny bug or a seed.","Some things change fast. Some change slow. Some do not change at all. Look and say what you see."],"adult":["At three and four, science is mostly careful looking. Children use their senses to notice details, compare two things, and say how something changed over time. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to talk about the differences between materials and changes they notice, and the early learning goal for the natural world asks them to explore and describe what they see.","Give your child the words of observation: before and now; first, then and now; the same and different; bigger and smaller. A hand lens, or magnifier, makes small details easy to see. Looking at the same thing a few times shows that some changes are fast, like ice melting in about an hour on a warm day, and some are slow, like a banana going brown over several days.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking melted ice has gone away, when it has turned into the water in the cup; thinking a changed thing is a new thing; noticing only big changes and missing small ones; and mixing up which picture came first. Each time, ask: what was it like before, and what is it like now?","In the lab your child slides time on to melt ice, spots three changes between a Before and a Now picture, and notices what stayed the same. Then they move a magnifier over an apple slice to find tiny things, step through an hour, a day and a week, and put pictures in first, then, now order."]},"familyTip":"Put an apple slice and an ice cube on two plates. Look at them now, after lunch and at bedtime. Ask what is different now from before, and draw or photo each one to compare.","keyIdea":"Scientists look closely and notice what changes. Say what it was like before and what it is like now. Some changes are fast, like ice melting. Some are slow, like a banana going brown.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Did it change? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🔄 It changed","🟰 Still the same"],"items":[["Ice turned to water",0],["Banana went brown",0],["Puddle got smaller",0],["Flower opened",0],["Teddy looks the same",1],["Ball looks the same",1],["Cup looks the same",1],["Spoon looks the same",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchange","caption":"Flagship lab: watch ice melt, spot what changed, look up close with a magnifier, see fast and slow changes, and order pictures first, then, now.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MLnph38ePa":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your guess was wrong. Is that OK?","choices":["Yes. A guess helps you start.","No. Never guess.","No. Guesses must be right."],"answer":0,"hint":"Do scientists always guess right the first time?","explain":"A guess does not have to be right. You guess, then you find out the real answer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which thing will stick to a magnet?","choices":["A steel paper clip","A teddy","A plastic cup"],"answer":0,"hint":"Magnets pull iron and steel.","explain":"The paper clip is steel, so it sticks. Cloth and plastic do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Shiny foil does not stick to a magnet. Why?","choices":["Foil is not iron or steel","It is too shiny","The magnet is asleep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shiny is not the thing that matters. What is foil made of?","explain":"Magnets pull iron and steel. Foil is a different metal, so it does not stick."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A ladybird sits on a leaf in front of you. How many spots has it got? How can you find out?","choices":["Look closely and count","Ask the ladybird","Guess and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see the spots? Then you do not need to ask.","explain":"You can see the spots, so look closely and count them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word often starts a wonder question?","choices":["Why","Yes","Look"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word asks for a reason?","explain":"\"Why\" asks for a reason. How and what if start wonder questions too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where do penguins live? You have never seen one. How can you find out?","choices":["Ask a grown-up or look in a book","Look out of the window","Try it in the bath"],"answer":0,"hint":"Penguins are far away. Who or what could tell you?","explain":"You cannot see penguins from here, so ask someone or look in a book."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You had {n} wonder questions. You found out the answer to 1. How many are still to find out?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 you found out away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Keep looking, trying and asking!","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Tom asks \"Why?\" lots of times. What do you think?","choices":["Good. Asking helps Tom learn.","Silly. Tom should stop.","Naughty."],"answer":0,"hint":"How do we find out new things?","explain":"Asking why is how you find out. It is a good thing to do."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"{n} things stuck to the magnet. You test 1 more steel pin. It sticks too. How many stuck now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Steel sticks, so count on 1 from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. The steel pin stuck, like the other steel things.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a wonder question?","choices":["Why do birds sing?","Birds can sing.","I see a bird."],"answer":0,"hint":"A question asks something. Look for why, how or what if.","explain":"\"Why do birds sing?\" asks for a reason. The other two tell you a thing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You test {n} things on a magnet. 2 of them stick. How many do not stick?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Start with the {n} things and take away the 2 that stick.","explain":"{n} take away 2 is {=n-2}. Testing each one showed you which stick.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Will my toy boat float? What is the best way to find out?","choices":["Put it in water and see","Look at it very hard","Stop wondering"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you try this one yourself?","explain":"You can try it! Putting the boat in water shows you if it floats."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You asked {n} why questions at the park and 1 more at home. How many why questions did you ask?","answer":"n+1","hint":"You asked {n}, then 1 more. Count on 1 from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Every question is a chance to find out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a snail. Do you wonder why it has a shell? That wonder is a question. Questions often start with why, how or what if.","Next, have a go at a guess. Maybe the shell keeps it safe. Your guess can be wrong. That is fine. A guess helps you start.","Then find out. You can look closely. You can try it and see. Or you can ask a grown-up, or look in a book.","Some things fool us. Shiny foil looks like it should stick to a magnet. But when you try it, it does not. Trying it showed you the real answer."],"adult":["This is the first step of scientific inquiry. A child notices something, asks a question, offers a guess and then looks for evidence. In England, Development Matters (2023) sets out what 3 and 4 year olds do. They use all their senses to explore natural materials. They explore how things work. They explore and talk about forces they can feel, such as a magnet's pull. In Communication and Language, they learn to understand why questions. The Characteristics of Effective Learning add having their own ideas, making predictions and testing them. In the US, the Head Start Early Learning Outcomes Framework goal P-SCI 4 says the child asks a question, gathers information, and makes predictions. At 36 to 48 months that means simple questions, adults as the main source of answers, and simple predictions with support.","Young children ask a great many questions. Chouinard (2007) recorded children aged 1 to 5 asking dozens of information-seeking questions an hour in everyday talk with parents. Frazier, Gelman and Wellman (2009) found that preschoolers notice when they get a non-answer: after a real explanation they tended to agree or ask a follow-up, but after a non-answer they often asked again or offered their own explanation. So \"why\" is usually a genuine request for a cause, not stalling.","Common mistakes: brushing off why questions with \"because it is\", which teaches that questions are a nuisance. Answering so fast that the child never makes a guess of their own. Treating a wrong guess as a failure, so the child stops guessing. A common slip in the science itself is believing that all shiny or metal things stick to a magnet. Only some metals, mainly iron and steel, are pulled; aluminium foil is not.","In the lab your child taps things in a garden to wonder about them. Then they sort asking sentences from telling ones. They make a guess before they hear each answer. They test seven things on a magnet, and they are stopped if they try to sort a thing before testing it. Last, they match questions to a way to find out: look, try it, or ask."]},"familyTip":"When your child asks why, try \"What do you think?\" before you answer, and welcome any guess. Then find out together: look closely, try it, or look it up. A fridge magnet and a few things from a drawer make a quick test of the shiny-foil surprise.","keyIdea":"Scientists start by wondering. You notice something, ask a why, how or what-if question, and have a go at a guess. Then you find out by looking closely, trying it, or asking someone. A guess does not have to be right. It is how finding out begins.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it asking, or telling? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["❓ Asking","💬 Telling"],"items":[["Why do cats purr?",0],["Cats have fur.",1],["How do plants drink?",0],["The grass is green.",1],["What if the sun went out?",0],["It is raining today.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcurio","caption":"Flagship lab: wonder about things in a garden, sort asking from telling, guess first, test things on a magnet, and choose how to find out.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sjbKGJB9vz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A ladybird has {a} spots on one wing and {b} spots on the other. How many spots should your drawing have?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the spots on both wings and add them.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so your drawing should have {=a+b} spots, the same as the real one.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam drew a flower in {n} minutes. What is a good thing to do next?","choices":["Look at the real flower again","Stop, it is finished","Add a face to the flower"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists check their drawing against the real thing.","explain":"Look again. You may spot something new, like the lines on the petals, and add it.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You want to draw a worm. Where should you look?","choices":["At a real worm","At a cartoon worm","Nowhere, I know what worms look like"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one shows what is really there?","explain":"Look at a real worm. A cartoon worm may have a face, but a real worm has no eyes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How many legs does a ladybird have?","choices":["6","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the legs on the real ladybird in the lab.","explain":"A ladybird has 6 legs, like every insect. So your drawing gets 6 legs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which drawing shows what is really there?","choices":["A spider with 8 legs and no face","A spider with 6 legs and a smile","A spider with shoes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the legs. Do real spiders smile?","explain":"A real spider has 8 legs and no smiley face, so that drawing shows what is really there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben forgets what colour the bee's stripes are. What should he do?","choices":["Look back at the real bee","Guess a colour","Leave the stripes out"],"answer":0,"hint":"The real bee is right there. Can he check?","explain":"He should look back. The stripes are black, so he can copy them and not guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do scientists draw what they see?","choices":["To show what is really there","To make it look funny","To fill the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a looking drawing is for.","explain":"A scientist's drawing shows what is really there, so other people can see what they found."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Lily's snail drawing has a shell, feelers and a smiley mouth. Which part should she rub out?","choices":["The smiley mouth","The shell","The feelers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part is not on a real snail?","explain":"A real snail has a shell and feelers, but no smiley mouth, so she should rub out the mouth."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A real spider has 8 legs. Your spider drawing has {n} legs so far. How many more legs do you need to draw?","answer":"8-n","hint":"Count on from your number up to 8.","explain":"8 take away {n} is {=8-n}, so you need {=8-n} more legs to match the real spider.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia looks at a real snail for {n} seconds. Then she draws legs on her snail. What should she do?","choices":["Look again and rub out the legs","Add more legs","Add a smiley face too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a real snail have legs? Look back at it.","explain":"A real snail has no legs. It slides on one big foot. Mia should look again and rub the legs out.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The real leaf is green. What colour should your leaf drawing be?","choices":["Green","My favourite colour","Purple, to look pretty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look back at the real leaf. What colour do you see?","explain":"Green, because a looking drawing uses the colours of the real thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to see the tiny hairs on a bee. What can help?","choices":["A magnifying glass","Closing my eyes","Standing far away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one makes small things look bigger?","explain":"A magnifying glass makes small parts look bigger, so you can see them and draw them."}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists draw to show what they see. They look hard at a real bug or plant. Then they draw it.","Look, then draw. Count the legs on a ladybird. It has 6. So your drawing gets 6 legs too.","Draw only what is there. A real snail has no legs and no smiley mouth. It has a shell and feelers. So leave out the made-up bits.","Then look again. You may spot more, like stripes on a bee. A magnifying glass makes small things look big."],"adult":["In England, one early years goal for The Natural World says children explore the world around them, make observations and draw pictures of animals and plants. In Key Stage 1, working scientifically includes observing closely and using simple equipment, such as a hand lens.","A science drawing is a record, not a decoration. The habit we want is a loop: look at the object, draw one part, look back to check it, and repeat. Counting parts, copying real colours and noticing shapes turn looking into evidence your child can talk about.","Common mistakes: drawing a symbol from memory, like a cartoon snail with a face, instead of the real one in front of them. Children also add legs, smiles or eyelashes that are not there, guess numbers and colours, and stop after one quick look. A gentle question helps: show me where you can see that on the real one.","In the lab your child counts the legs on a ladybird and gives their drawing the same number. They rub out made-up parts on a snail, colour a flower by looking back, and move a magnifier over a bee to find wings, stripes and feelers. Last, they pick which drawings show what is really there."]},"familyTip":"Pick up one leaf, shell or bug on a walk and draw it together, side by side. Before you start, count something: how many points, legs or lines? Halfway through, stop and say, let us look again. Then ask your child to show you one thing they saw on the real one that they had not drawn yet.","keyIdea":"A scientist draws what is really there. First look at the real thing, then draw, then look again. Count the legs, copy the real colours, and leave out made-up things like smiley faces. A magnifying glass helps you see small parts you missed.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which parts are on a real snail, and which are made up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["On a real snail","Made up"],"items":[["a shell",0],["feelers",0],["one big foot",0],["a slime trail",0],["legs",1],["a smiley mouth",1],["eyelashes",1],["shoes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglook","caption":"Flagship lab: count legs and draw the same number, rub out made-up parts, colour by looking back and use a magnifier.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_y0ffQfjUli":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does a wind-up toy walk?","choices":["Its spring was wound up","It wants to walk","It is magic"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did you do to the key before it walked?","explain":"Turning the key winds a spring inside. When you let go, the spring makes the toy walk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you open a peg?","choices":["Squeeze its ends","Pull its mouth apart","Blow on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Press the two long ends together.","explain":"Squeezing the ends opens the mouth. Let go and the spring shuts it tight."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which part of a zip do you pull?","choices":["The little tab","The coat","The bottom of the zip"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the small part you can hold.","explain":"Pull the little tab. It moves the slider, and the slider joins the two sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The toy stops walking. How can it walk again?","choices":["Wind it up again","Wait and it will go by itself","Paint it a new colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"What made it walk the first time?","explain":"The spring has run down. Turn the key again to wind it up, and it will walk again."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You wind the toy {a} times, then {b} times. How many more turns was the second go?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Start at {a} and count up to {b}.","explain":"From {a} up to {b} is {=b-a} more turns, so the second go walks further.","params":{"a":[1,2],"b":[3,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"There are {n} socks. You have pegged {k} on the line. How many still need a peg?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} and take away the {k} that are hanging.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} socks still to peg.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You do not know how a box opens. What is a good thing to do?","choices":["Try one part and see what it does","Give up","Hit it hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did you find out what the tap did?","explain":"Try one part gently, watch what happens, then try the next part. That is how you find out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You press a light switch. What happens?","choices":["The light goes on or off","The door opens","Water comes out"],"answer":0,"hint":"A switch is like the torch button.","explain":"A light switch turns the light on, or off if it was on. Each switch does one job."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of a torch turns the light on?","choices":["The button","The handle","The glass at the front"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part moves when you press it?","explain":"The button is the switch. Press it and the light comes on. Press it again and it goes off."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You turn the toy's key {a} times. Then you turn it {b} more times. How many turns is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more turns.","explain":"{a} turns and {b} more turns make {=a+b} turns. More turns wind the spring tighter.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {n} socks to hang up. Each sock needs 1 peg. How many pegs do you need?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 peg for every sock.","explain":"One peg for each sock, so {n} socks need {n} pegs.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You turn the tap more. What happens?","choices":["More water comes out","Less water comes out","The water stops"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning back makes the water stop. What does turning more do?","explain":"Turning the tap handle more lets more water out. Turning it back makes less, then none."}],"explain":{"kid":["Things have parts. Each part has a job. A torch has a button. Press it and the light comes on. Press it again and the light goes off.","Some parts turn. Turn a tap a little, and a little water comes out. Turn it more, and more water comes out. Turn it back, and it stops.","A wind-up toy has a key. Turn the key and a spring inside gets tight. Let go and the toy walks. Turn the key more, and it walks further. It is not magic. The spring makes it go.","How does a thing work? Try one part. Watch what it does. Then try the next part. Ask a grown-up first if it is hot, sharp or plugged in."],"adult":["Development Matters, the guidance for England's Early Years Foundation Stage, lists 'Explore how things work' for 3- and 4-year-olds in Understanding the World. The skill is noticing cause and effect: I do this to this part, and that happens. It feeds later science, such as pushes and pulls, simple circuits and asking questions, and early design and technology, where children take things apart to see how they work.","Pick safe things with one clear moving part: a torch switch, a tap, a wind-up toy, a zip, a clothes peg, a jar lid, a door handle. Let your child try first, then talk about it: what did you do, which part moved, what happened. Words to use: press, turn, twist, pull, squeeze, wind, button, handle, spring, switch, on, off, more, less.","Common mistakes are explanations like 'the toy wants to walk' or 'it's magic'. Young children often give objects feelings or treat a result as magic. Gently replace these with the part that caused it: 'you wound the spring'. Another slip is thinking a toy stops because it is tired or sad, not because the spring has run down. Be careful to keep this hands-on but safe: no plug sockets, hot taps or sharp blades.","In the lab your child presses a torch button on and off, turns a tap a little and a lot to fill a cup, winds a toy once and then three times to see it go further, pulls a zip up by its tab, and squeezes a peg to hang three socks."]},"familyTip":"Go on a 'how does it work?' hunt at home. Find three things with a part that moves: a light switch, a bottle top, a peg. For each one, ask 'What do you think will happen?' before your child tries it.","keyIdea":"Everyday things have parts, and each part does a job. Press a torch button and the light comes on. Turn a tap handle more and more water comes out. Wind a toy's key and its spring makes it go. Pull a zip's tab and the two sides join. Squeeze a peg and its mouth opens. To find out how something works, try one part at a time and watch what happens: that is cause and effect.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Do you press it or turn it? Drag each thing into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Press it","Turn it"],"items":[["Light switch",0],["Doorbell",0],["Torch button",0],["Lift button",0],["Tap handle",1],["Jar lid",1],["Key on a wind-up toy",1],["Bottle top",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagworks","caption":"Flagship lab: press a torch button, turn a tap a little and a lot, wind up a toy, pull a zip and squeeze a peg to find each part's job.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Hw70LI5xza":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Water drops on glass are…","choices":["an observation","proof the jar is sad","a feeling"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask what your eyes actually saw on the glass.","explain":"Drops are sensed; sadness is a story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Best evidence a plant grew?","choices":["height 4 cm then 7 cm","the plant looks proud","I feel it grew"],"answer":0,"hint":"Prefer a number you measured with a ruler.","explain":"Measured height change is observation evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which can you check by looking?","choices":["The leaf is green","The leaf is jealous","The leaf wants friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Colour is something you can see and check.","explain":"Green is checkable; jealousy is not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Observation and interpretation…","choices":["are different and both useful","are the same","should never be written"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists use both — labelled clearly.","explain":"Keep them distinct so claims rest on evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Thermometer shows {t} °C. What temperature did you observe?","answer":"t","hint":"Read the number shown on the instrument.","explain":"{t} °C is the observation from the thermometer.","params":{"t":[10,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Count {a} red blocks and {b} blue. How many blocks observed in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the red and blue counts you can see.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} blocks observed.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why separate observation from interpretation?","choices":["so claims rest on what was sensed","so you never guess","because observations are wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence underpins claims.","explain":"Guesses are fine when labelled; claims need observations."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: talk about a dog bark carefully.","items":["say what you heard (the bark)","say your guess (maybe it wants food)","keep the guess labelled as a guess"],"hint":"Start from the sense data you heard.","explain":"Report the bark, then the guess, clearly separated."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"The ice is melting\" is…","choices":["an observation","an interpretation","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see or measure it?","explain":"Melting is what you sense — an observation."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"The dog was angry\" after a bark is…","choices":["an interpretation","a pure observation","a measurement"],"answer":0,"hint":"You heard a bark; anger is a meaning you added.","explain":"Anger is an interpretation layered on the bark."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A plant was {a} cm, now {b} cm. Growth in cm?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract start height from new height.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} cm — an observation used as evidence.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[7,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You record {n} beeps. How many did you hear?","answer":"n","hint":"The count is the observation.","explain":"You heard {n} beeps — that count is the observation.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order steps to check a claim carefully.","items":["write what you observed","write your guess separately","see if the observation supports the guess"],"hint":"Facts first, then meaning.","explain":"Separate observation from interpretation, then test the link."}],"explain":{"kid":["An observation is what you see, hear, or measure.","An interpretation is a guess about why or what it means.","\"The dog barked\" is what you heard. \"It was angry\" is a guess.","Good science claims need observations as evidence."],"adult":["Working scientifically (KS1/KS2): an observation is what you see, hear or measure. An interpretation is a guess about why. Words: observe, evidence, claim, measure.","Common mistakes: calling a feeling an observation; using \"I feel it grew\" as evidence; treating observation and interpretation as the same.","In the lab your child sorts clues, names water drops (not a homesick jar), rejects angry as an observation, and links growth to measured height."]},"familyTip":"Look out the window together. Ask: 'What do you see?' then 'What do you think is going on?' Label each sentence as see or think.","keyIdea":"An observation is what you see, hear, or measure. An interpretation is a guess about why or what it means. Keep them separate so claims rest on evidence.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Observation or interpretation? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Observation","Interpretation"],"items":[["The ice is melting",0],["The ice feels sad",1],["I hear a beep",0],["The machine is lonely",1],["The leaf is green",0],["The leaf is jealous",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagobsvs","caption":"Flagship lab: sort see/hear from guesses, name jar drops, reject angry-as-observation, match height evidence, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XirhnAB6Ye":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is a science question?","choices":["Do snails like wet places?","Which colour is the best?","What is your name?"],"answer":0,"hint":"A science question is about how the world works, and we can find out.","explain":"We can find out if snails like wet places by watching them. Which colour is best is only what you like."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Do ice cubes melt faster in the sun or in the shade? Best way to find out?","choices":["Test it with two ice cubes","Just guess","Ask which is nicer"],"answer":0,"hint":"We can try this ourselves.","explain":"Put one ice cube in the sun and one in the shade, then look. That test shows which melts faster."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many moons does Jupiter have? Best way to find out?","choices":["Look it up in a book","Test it","Count them from the garden"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jupiter is very far away.","explain":"Jupiter is too far to test, and most of its moons are too small to see from a garden. We look it up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What does a caterpillar turn into? Best way to find out?","choices":["Watch it for many weeks","Look at it once","Test it with a ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"The change is slow, so one look will not show it.","explain":"A caterpillar changes slowly into a butterfly. We have to watch it again and again to see it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which question can science NOT answer?","choices":["Which song is the nicest?","Do plants need water?","Where does a puddle go?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that is about what you like.","explain":"Nicest is what you like, and people like different songs. We can find out the other two."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben asks {a} questions in the garden. Then he asks {b} more at the park. How many questions did he ask?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the garden questions and the park questions.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} questions. Asking lots of questions is how scientists start.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ella guesses that the big ball bounces highest. What should she do next?","choices":["Drop the balls to find out","Say she is right","Pick a different guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"A guess is a start, not an answer.","explain":"A guess is not finding out. Ella should test it by dropping each ball from the same height."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Zoe looks at her frog tank once a week. She looks for {a} weeks, then {b} more weeks. How many times did she look?","answer":"a+b","hint":"One look each week. Add the weeks together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} looks. Frogspawn changes slowly, so Zoe needs to look many times.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which ball bounces highest? How can we find out?","choices":["Just guess","Drop each ball and look","Ask which ball is the nicest"],"answer":1,"hint":"We can try this ourselves, right now.","explain":"Drop each ball from the same height and watch how high it bounces. That is a test."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"How does a bean seed grow into a plant? How can we find out?","choices":["Watch it for many days","Look at it once","Pick the best bean"],"answer":0,"hint":"A plant grows slowly. One look is not enough.","explain":"A bean grows a little each day. We watch it for many days to see the root, the shoot and the leaves."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia looks at her bean plant on {a} days. Then she looks on {b} more days. On how many days did she look?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the first days, then count on the extra days.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} days. Watching on many days shows how the plant changes.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How far away is the Sun? How can we find out?","choices":["Measure it with a ruler","Look it up in a book","Watch it for a day"],"answer":1,"hint":"The Sun is much too far away to measure or test.","explain":"We cannot reach the Sun. Scientists have found out how far it is, so we look it up in a book or ask an expert."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam drops the tennis ball {a} times and the sponge ball {a} times. How many drops is that in all?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Add the tennis ball drops and the sponge ball drops.","explain":"{a} + {a} = {=2*a} drops. Doing the test more than once helps Sam be sure.","params":{"a":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists ask questions. How does it work? Why does it happen? Where does it go? A science question is one we can find out the answer to.","Some answers we find by watching. Frogspawn turns into tadpoles, then frogs. We have to look again and again for weeks to see it.","Some answers we find with a test. Which ball bounces highest? Drop each ball from the same height and look. Some answers are too far away to watch or test, like how far the Moon is. Then we look in a book or ask an expert.","Some questions are not for science. Which colour is best? That is what you like, and people like different things."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 8. This matches KS1 Working scientifically in England: ask simple questions and see that they can be answered in different ways. It also fits NGSS K-2-ETS1-1, which asks children to ask questions and gather information. It builds on Wondering and asking why and on Observation vs Interpretation.","The three ways here are the KS1 types of enquiry in simple words. Watching is observing over time, as with frogspawn. Testing is a simple comparative or fair test, as with balls dropped from the same height. Looking it up is research using books, websites or an expert, as with the distance to the Moon.","Common mistakes: thinking a guess is the same as finding out; looking only once at something that changes slowly; trying to test things that are too big or far away; and treating 'which is best?' as a science question. Be careful to praise good questions, not only right answers.","In the lab your child taps garden things to ask questions, looks at a frog tank four times over about 12 weeks, drops three balls from the same height, looks up the Moon, sorts three questions by the way to find out, and spots the question science cannot answer."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, take turns to ask one how or why question each. Then ask: how could we find out? Watch it, test it, or look it up?","keyIdea":"Scientists ask how, why and where questions about the world. There are different ways to find out the answers: watch something change over time, do a test, or look it up in a book or ask an expert. A question about what you like best is not one science can answer.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How can we find out? Drag each question to watch, test or look it up, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["watch it","test it","look it up"],"items":[["What does a tadpole turn into?",0],["Which paper towel soaks up the most water?",1],["How far away is the Sun?",2],["How does the Moon change over a month?",0],["Does a toy car go faster down a steep ramp?",1],["What did dinosaurs eat?",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsciq","caption":"Flagship lab: ask questions in a garden, then find out by watching frogspawn, testing bouncing balls and looking up the Moon.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_vJO5Bxk4z-":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The view through the lens is blurry. What should you do?","choices":["Move the lens until it looks sharp","Press the lens flat on the leaf","Stop, lenses are always blurry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try a little closer, then a little farther.","explain":"A view is sharp only at the right distance. Move the lens a bit until the details are clear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can you look at the Sun through a hand lens?","choices":["No, never","Yes, for a moment","Yes, on a cloudy day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about keeping your eyes safe.","explain":"Never look at the Sun, with or without a lens. It can hurt your eyes. A lens in sunshine can also start a fire."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Two leaves look the same from far away. How do you check?","choices":["Look closely at both, side by side","Say they are the same","Pick the bigger one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small differences need close looking.","explain":"Look at both with a lens, side by side. Then you can say what is the same and what is different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a texture word?","choices":["rough","red","round"],"answer":0,"hint":"Texture is how something feels or looks like it feels.","explain":"Rough is a texture. Red is a colour and round is a shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You want to see how long an ice cube takes to melt. Which tool do you use?","choices":["A timer","A ruler","A hand lens"],"answer":0,"hint":"This 'how long' is about time.","explain":"A timer measures time. A ruler measures length and a lens shows small details."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Under the lens you count {a} ants on a stone. Then {b} walk away. How many are left?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take away the ants that walked off.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b} ants on the stone.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A lens makes a seed look {z} times as long. The seed is {s} mm long. How long does it look?","answer":"s*z","hint":"Add the seed's length {z} times.","explain":"{z} lots of {s} mm is {=s*z} mm. The seed itself is still {s} mm long.","params":{"s":[1,5],"z":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia says the lens made the ant bigger. What is right?","choices":["The ant only looks bigger","The ant really grew","The ant got heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take the lens away. What size is the ant?","explain":"A lens changes how the ant looks to you, not the ant itself. Without the lens it is the same small ant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which is the best way to describe a feather?","choices":["Grey, soft and long","Pretty","A feather thing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use colour, texture and size words.","explain":"Grey is a colour, soft is a texture and long is a size. Those words tell someone what you saw."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A hand lens shows tiny hairs on a leaf. Were the hairs there before you looked?","choices":["Yes, they were too small to see","No, the lens made them","Only while the lens is there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a lens change the leaf, or only how it looks to your eyes?","explain":"The hairs were always there. The lens only makes them look bigger so you can see them."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which words tell a friend most about a shell?","choices":["It is brown and bumpy","It is nice","It is a good one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick words about colour, shape or how it feels.","explain":"Brown is a colour and bumpy is a texture. Nice and good say what you think, not what you see."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"With a lens you count {a} spots on one ladybird and {b} spots on another. How many spots altogether?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count on from the first ladybird's spots.","explain":"{a} spots and {b} spots make {=a+b} spots altogether.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to know how long a worm is. Which tool do you use?","choices":["A ruler","A hand lens","A timer"],"answer":0,"hint":"This 'how long' is about length, not time.","explain":"A ruler measures length. A lens shows small details and a timer measures time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Leaf 1 has {a} veins on one side. Leaf 2 has {b} veins on one side. How many more does leaf 1 have?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the difference: count on from the smaller number to the bigger one.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so leaf 1 has {=a-b} more veins.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A hand lens is a round glass you hold. It makes small things look bigger. You can see tiny bits you could not see before. The tiny bits were there all the time. The lens does not make them grow. It just helps your eyes.","If it looks blurry, move the lens a bit. Go closer or farther until it looks sharp.","Say what you see. Use words for colour, shape, size and feel. Say bumpy, round or stripy. Nice and good do not tell what you see.","Look at two things side by side to find what is the same and what is not. Use a ruler to find how long. Use a timer to see how much time. Never look at the Sun with a lens."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. National Curriculum in England, Key Stage 1 working scientifically: observing closely, using simple equipment, and identifying and classifying. Children use observations to describe, compare and group things.","A hand lens is a convex lens. Held close to the eye, with the object brought in until the image is sharp, it gives an enlarged view, typically 2 to 10 times. The object is unchanged: the lens reveals detail already present. If the object is too close or too far from the lens, the image blurs. Good observation uses specific vocabulary for observable properties (colour, shape, size, texture, pattern) and records similarities and differences when two objects are compared side by side. Choosing equipment means matching it to the question: a hand lens for fine detail, a ruler for length, a timer for time.","Common mistakes: thinking the lens makes the object itself bigger; pressing the lens flat on the object or giving up when the view is blurry; describing with opinion words such as nice or pretty; calling two things the same after a glance; mixing up how long (length) with how long (time); and looking at the Sun through a lens, which can damage eyes.","In the lab your child drags a lens over a leaf to find veins, edge hairs and an aphid, adjusts it until the view is sharp, chooses looking words for a shell, compares two similar leaves, sorts questions onto tools and picks the safe way to use a lens."]},"familyTip":"Take a hand lens outside and look at a leaf, some bark or a stone together. Ask for three looking words each, like bumpy, grey or stripy. Then find two leaves that look the same and look closely for one difference. Remind your child never to look at the Sun through the lens.","keyIdea":"Scientists look closely. A hand lens makes small things look bigger, so you can see details that were there all along. Move it until the view is sharp, then describe what you see with looking words: colour, shape, size, texture and pattern. Use your observations to compare and identify things, and pick the right tool for each question.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which tool answers each question? Drag each card into a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Hand lens","Ruler","Timer"],"items":[["See the tiny hairs on a leaf",0],["How long is my pencil?",1],["How long does the ice take to melt?",2],["Count the legs on an ant",0],["How tall is my plant?",1],["How long can I hop?",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghlens","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a hand lens over a leaf to find tiny things, make it sharp, describe a shell, compare two leaves, match tools and stay safe.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Wa44s-f8Ws":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Does ice melt faster in the sun or the shade? What should change?","choices":["Only the place: sun or shade","The place and the size of the ice","The size of the ice only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change only the thing in the question.","explain":"The question is about sun or shade, so only the place changes. The ice cubes stay the same size."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One ice cube is big and one is small. Is the sun-or-shade test fair?","choices":["No, the ice cubes must be the same size","Yes, it is still a test","Yes, if we wait a long time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would the small cube melt first anyway?","explain":"A small cube melts faster, so you could not tell if the sun did it. Use the same size cubes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Towel: 9 squares. Tissue: 6 squares. Shiny paper: 1 square. Which soaked up the least?","choices":["Shiny paper","Towel","Tissue"],"answer":0,"hint":"The least is the smallest number.","explain":"1 is the smallest number, so shiny paper soaked up the least water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why do we write our results down?","choices":["So we do not forget what we saw","So the test is quicker","So the guess is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about answering the question later.","explain":"A table keeps what we saw, so we can look back and answer the question."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Each cup gets {w} spoons of water. How many spoons go in the third cup for a fair test?","answer":"w","hint":"Fair means the water is the same for every cup.","explain":"The third cup gets {w} spoons too. Only the paper should be different.","params":{"w":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ali tests two balls. He drops one from a chair and one from a table. What is wrong?","choices":["The height changed as well as the ball","Nothing, it is fair","He used two balls"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count how many things are different between the two drops.","explain":"Two things changed: the ball and the height. Drop both from the same height."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your guess was wrong. What does that mean?","choices":["You learned something from the test","You are bad at science","The test must be wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists guess wrong a lot. What do they do next?","explain":"A wrong guess is fine. The test shows what really happens, so you change your idea."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"In the sun, the ice melted in {s} minutes. In the shade it took 10 minutes longer. How many minutes in the shade?","answer":"s+10","hint":"Add 10 to the time in the sun.","explain":"{s} + 10 = {=s+10} minutes. The ice in the shade took longer to melt.","params":{"s":[10,30]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You test which paper soaks up the most water. What should change?","choices":["Only the kind of paper","The paper and the amount of water","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change one thing only, the thing you want to find out about.","explain":"Only the paper changes. If the water changed too, you could not tell which one made the difference."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Paper A: the water went up {a} squares. Paper B: it went up {b} squares. How many more squares on paper B?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Count on from the smaller number to the bigger one.","explain":"{b} take away {a} is {=b-a}, so paper B got {=b-a} more squares. Paper B soaked up more.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[6,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You drop a ball from {h} blocks high. For a fair test, how many blocks high do you drop the next ball?","answer":"h","hint":"In a fair test, everything except the ball stays the same.","explain":"Drop it from {h} blocks again. Then only the ball is different, so the test is fair.","params":{"h":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam guessed the shiny paper would win. The towel soaked up most. What should Sam say?","choices":["The towel soaks up most, so I will change my idea","Shiny paper wins because I guessed it","The test went wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which tells you the answer: the guess or the test?","explain":"The test result is the answer. Changing your idea after a test is what scientists do."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which one is something you saw, not a guess?","choices":["The water went up 9 squares on the towel","I think the towel will win","Towels are always best"],"answer":0,"hint":"What you saw has a number or a fact from the test.","explain":"9 squares is what you saw and counted. 'I think' is a guess, and 'always' was not tested."}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists find out by testing. First you ask a question, like: which paper soaks up the most water? You can make a guess. Then you try it and see.","Change just one thing. In the paper test, only the paper changes. Each strip gets the same water, the same size and the same time. That makes it a fair test.","Look closely. Count how far the water went up each strip. Write the numbers down so you do not forget them.","Now use the numbers to answer. The towel got 9 squares, the most. If you guessed shiny paper, that is fine. The test helps you change your idea."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. In England, Key Stage 1 science asks children to do simple tests and to observe closely. They also gather and record data and use what they see to suggest answers to questions. The idea of changing one thing at a time grows into the fair tests of Years 3 and 4.","A test has four parts. Ask a question you can try out. Make a guess, which is a prediction. Change one thing and keep the rest the same. Then look, record and say what the results show. In the lab the paper is the one thing that changes. In the ice test it is the place, sun or shade.","Common mistakes: changing two things at once, such as a bigger strip and a different paper, so you cannot tell which one mattered. Keeping a guess when the result says something else. Saying what you expected to see, not what you saw. Thinking a wrong guess means you failed. A wrong guess plus a test is how science works.","In the lab your child guesses, then dips three papers and watches the water climb. They fix an unfair setup with too little water, a wider strip and a longer time. They count wet squares into a table and pick the best paper from the numbers. Last, they make two ice cubes the same size and watch which melts first."]},"familyTip":"Ask a question you can test at home, such as which cloth dries a wet plate best. Guess first. Use the same plate and the same amount of water for each cloth. Look, count or compare, and then ask: what did the test show? Praise a wrong guess that your child changes because of the result.","keyIdea":"A simple test answers a question by trying it out. Change one thing, keep everything else the same, look closely at what happens and write it down. Then use what you saw to answer the question, even if it is not what you guessed.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"In a simple test, what changes and what stays the same? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Change it","Keep it the same"],"items":[["Kind of paper (paper test)",0],["Amount of water (paper test)",1],["Size of the strip (paper test)",1],["Sun or shade (ice test)",0],["Size of the ice cube (ice test)",1],["Time we wait (ice test)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsimtest","caption":"Flagship lab: guess, dip three papers, fix an unfair test, count wet squares, answer from the table, then run a sun-or-shade ice test.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qgb76wHN2X":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which soil is best for beans? What do you measure?","choices":["The height of each plant after 2 weeks","The kind of soil","The amount of water"],"answer":0,"hint":"What number tells you how well each plant grew?","explain":"You measure plant height. The soil is what you change and the water stays the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ella puts one plant in the sun with lots of water and one in the shade with a little. Why is this unfair?","choices":["Light and water both changed","Plants need water","She used two plants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the variables that are different.","explain":"Two variables changed, light and water, so she cannot tell which one made the difference."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Cars roll down a ramp onto carpet, wood and tiles. Which must stay the same?","choices":["The car and the ramp height","The floor surface","How far the car rolls"],"answer":0,"hint":"The surface is changed and the distance is measured. What is left?","explain":"Keep the car and the ramp height the same. Change the surface and measure how far it rolls."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why do scientists repeat a test?","choices":["To check a result and spot one that went wrong","To make the test take longer","To change the answer they get"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the 3.6 s drop.","explain":"Repeating shows whether a result is typical. A reading far from the others can then be done again."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You drop every parachute from {h} cm. What height do you use for the last one?","answer":"h","hint":"Height is a variable you keep the same.","explain":"Use {h} cm again. Only the canopy size changes, so the height stays at {h} cm.","params":{"h":[100,200]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Results: 10 cm canopy 1.2 s, 15 cm 1.8 s, 20 cm 2.4 s. What is the pattern?","choices":["The bigger the canopy, the longer it takes to fall","The bigger the canopy, the faster it falls","Size makes no difference"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the times from the smallest canopy to the biggest.","explain":"The times go up as the canopy gets bigger, so bigger parachutes take longer to fall."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A test has {k} variables you keep the same, 1 you change and 1 you measure. How many variables is that in all?","answer":"k+2","hint":"Add the one you change and the one you measure.","explain":"{k} + 1 + 1 = {=k+2} variables. Only 1 of them is changed on purpose.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Tom tests which cloth dries fastest. He keeps the cloths the same too. What went wrong?","choices":["The cloth is what he tests, so it must change","Nothing, it is fair","He should measure the cloth"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a fair test one variable must change. Which one?","explain":"The kind of cloth is the variable to change. If it stays the same there is nothing to compare."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You test which ball bounces highest. Which variable do you change?","choices":["The kind of ball","The height of the bounce","The height you drop it from"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks about the kind of ball.","explain":"You change the kind of ball, measure the bounce height and keep the drop height the same."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a fair soil test, the bean in compost grew {b} cm and the bean in sand grew {a} cm. How many cm taller is the compost bean?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Take the smaller height away from the bigger one.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} cm. Only the soil changed, so the soil made the difference.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[10,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Pot A gets {w} ml of water a day. For a fair soil test, how many ml does pot B get?","answer":"w","hint":"Only the soil changes. What about the water?","explain":"Pot B also gets {w} ml. Water is a variable you keep the same, so only the soil is different.","params":{"w":[20,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Parachute A: small, dropped from 2 m. Parachute B: big, dropped from 3 m. B is slower. What can you say?","choices":["Nothing sure, because two things changed","Big parachutes fall more slowly","Height does not matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count how many variables are different between A and B.","explain":"Size and height both changed, so you cannot tell which one made B slower. Drop both from the same height."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Times for the same drop: 2.4 s, 2.5 s and 3.6 s. What should you do?","choices":["Repeat the 3.6 s drop","Use 3.6 s because it is the biggest","Stop, because the test failed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which time is far away from the other two?","explain":"3.6 s does not fit with 2.4 s and 2.5 s, so something went wrong in that drop, like a draught. Do it again."}],"explain":{"kid":["Does a bigger parachute fall more slowly? To find out, you need a fair test. The things that can change in a test are called variables.","Change one variable. Here that is the size of the parachute. Measure one variable: the time it takes to fall. Keep all the other variables the same, like the height and the load.","If you change two things, the test cannot answer the question. Say B is bigger and dropped from higher up. If B takes longer, which change did it? You cannot tell.","Write your results in a table and look for a pattern. Do each drop three times. If one time is far from the others, something went wrong. Do that drop again."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. In England, Years 3 and 4 science asks children to set up simple practical enquiries, comparative and fair tests. They also make careful measurements in standard units, record results in tables and draw simple conclusions. Repeat readings are formally taught in Years 5 and 6; here they are a first taste.","The words to use are variable, change, measure and keep the same. In secondary school these become the independent, dependent and control variables. A comparative test compares things, such as which ball bounces highest. It becomes a fair test when everything except the thing being compared is kept the same.","Common mistakes: changing two variables at once; mixing up what you change with what you measure; keeping the tested variable the same as well; trusting a single reading; and calling a test fair just because each thing got something, such as water, when the amounts differ.","In the lab your child sorts five parachute variables into change, measure and keep the same. They drop a big parachute from higher up and see why the result proves nothing until the heights match. They record 10, 15 and 20 cm canopies in a table and pick the pattern. Then they repeat a drop to catch an odd reading and plan a fair bean test."]},"familyTip":"Make paper parachutes from a bin bag or tissue paper with the same string and the same paper clip. Ask: what will we change, what will we measure, and what must stay the same? Drop each one from the same step, time it on a phone, write the times in a table, and do every drop three times.","keyIdea":"A fair test changes one variable, measures one variable and keeps every other variable the same. Then any difference in the results must come from the one thing you changed. Repeating a test helps you spot a reading that went wrong.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Question: which soil is best for growing beans? Sort each variable. Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Change","Measure","Keep the same"],"items":[["Kind of soil",0],["Height of the plant after 2 weeks",1],["Amount of water each day",2],["Light from the window",2],["Size of the pot",2],["Kind of bean seed",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfairvar","caption":"Flagship lab: sort parachute variables, fix a two-change test, record fall times, repeat to catch an odd result, plan a bean test.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nUnowllzaN":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Ava reads a measuring jug while looking down at it from above. Her reading will be…","choices":["too high","too low","right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the dotted sight line in the lab. Where did it cross the scale when the eye was above the water?","explain":"From above, your sight line meets the scale above the water, so you read too much. Crouch so your eye is level with the water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A thermometer has a number every 10 °C and 4 small marks between the numbers. The red line stops on small mark number {k} above {=10*h} °C. What is the temperature in °C?","answer":"10*h+2*k","hint":"4 marks make 5 gaps between two numbers 10 apart.","explain":"Each mark is 10 ÷ 5 = 2 °C. {k} marks is {=2*k} °C, so the temperature is {=10*h} + {=2*k} = {=10*h+2*k} °C.","params":{"h":[1,3],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which tool and unit would you use to find the mass of an apple?","choices":["Scales, in grams (g)","A ruler, in centimetres (cm)","A measuring jug, in millilitres (ml)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mass is how heavy something is.","explain":"Scales measure mass in grams. A ruler measures length and a jug measures the volume of a liquid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A thermometer reads 31, 36, 38, 39, 40 and 40 °C, ten seconds apart. What should you write down?","choices":["40 °C","31 °C","37 °C, the average of all six"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which readings were taken after the line stopped rising?","explain":"The early readings were taken while it was still warming up. The two at the end match, so write 40 °C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Jo puts an empty cup on the scales and presses Zero. Then she pours in juice. The scales show {j} g. How many grams of juice are there?","answer":"j","hint":"What did pressing Zero with the cup on do?","explain":"Zero made the cup count as nothing, so the scales show only the juice: {j} g.","params":{"j":[150,260]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why should you read a measurement a second time?","choices":["To check you did not make a slip","To make the number bigger","Because the first reading is always wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does it tell you if the two readings match?","explain":"Matching readings show you did not slip. If they differ, something changed or went wrong, so read again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"There are 4 small marks between 0 and 100 ml. Leo says each mark is 25 ml. What is his slip?","choices":["4 marks make 5 gaps, so each is 20 ml","Each mark is 1 ml","He is right, 100 ÷ 4 = 25"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the gaps between 0 and 100, not the marks.","explain":"The 4 marks split 0 to 100 into 5 equal gaps. 100 ÷ 5 = 20, so each mark is worth 20 ml."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What does a data logger do?","choices":["Takes readings by itself at set times and can draw a graph","Makes the water warm up faster","Picks the right answer for you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the graph in the lab that drew a point every 10 seconds.","explain":"A data logger records a reading at set times, such as every 10 seconds. The graph shows when the temperature has stopped changing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your ruler has a small gap before the 0. Where should the end of your pencil go?","choices":["On the 0 mark","At the very end of the ruler","On the 1 mark, so it is easy to see"],"answer":0,"hint":"The numbers count up from the 0 mark, not from the edge of the ruler.","explain":"Put the end on 0. Then the number at the tip is the length. From the edge of the ruler, the tip reads less than the true length."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ribbon starts at the {a} cm mark and ends at the {b} cm mark. How long is it in cm?","answer":"b-a","hint":"It does not start at 0, so take the start number away from the end number.","explain":"The ribbon covers the gap from {a} to {b}. {b} − {a} = {=b-a}, so it is {=b-a} cm long.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[11,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A jug has a number every 100 ml and 4 small marks between the numbers. The water is on small mark number {k} above the {=100*h} line. How many ml is that?","answer":"100*h+20*k","hint":"4 marks make 5 gaps. What is 100 shared into 5 gaps?","explain":"Each small mark is 100 ÷ 5 = 20 ml. {k} marks is {=20*k} ml, and {=100*h} + {=20*k} = {=100*h+20*k} ml.","params":{"h":[1,4],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The empty bowl shows {b} g. With sugar in it, the scales show {t} g. How many grams of sugar are there?","answer":"t-b","hint":"The scales are showing the bowl and the sugar together.","explain":"Take away the bowl: {t} − {b} = {=t-b} g of sugar. Pressing Zero with the empty bowl on would have shown {=t-b} g straight away.","params":{"b":[120,160],"t":[300,400]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A thermometer goes into warm water. When should you read it?","choices":["When the red line has stopped moving","The moment it goes in","While the line is still going up"],"answer":0,"hint":"The thermometer has to warm up to the water's temperature first.","explain":"The line rises while the thermometer warms up. Read it once it stops, then read again to check the readings match."}],"explain":{"kid":["Scientists measure with tools. A ruler measures length in centimetres (cm). A measuring jug measures how much liquid there is, in millilitres (ml). Scales measure mass in grams (g). A thermometer measures how hot or cold it is, in degrees Celsius (°C). Always write the unit after the number.","Start at zero. Many rulers have a small gap before the 0. Put one end of the thing on the 0. Then read the number at the other end. If it starts at 2 cm and ends at 9 cm, it is 9 − 2 = 7 cm long.","Work out what one small mark is worth. A jug may have 4 small marks between 200 and 300. Four marks make 5 gaps. 100 ÷ 5 = 20, so each mark is 20 ml. Then crouch down so your eye is level with the water. If you look down from above, you read too much.","Put the empty bowl on the scales and press Zero. Now the scales show only what goes in the bowl. Give a thermometer time. Its red line goes up, then stops. Read it when it stops, then read it again. Readings that match are ones you can trust. A data logger is a machine that takes the readings for you and draws a graph."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This is NC Years 3 and 4 working scientifically. Children make careful observations and take accurate measurements in standard units. They use a range of equipment, including thermometers and data loggers. Key words: standard unit, scale, interval (what one gap on the scale is worth), reading, accurate, repeat reading and data logger.","Common mistakes: measuring from the end of the ruler, not the 0 mark. Counting each small mark as 1. Dividing by the number of marks, not the number of gaps: 4 marks between 200 and 300 make 5 gaps of 20 ml, not 4 of 25. Reading a jug from above, which gives a reading that is too high (parallax). Forgetting the mass of the bowl. Reading a thermometer before it settles. Leaving off the unit.","Accurate means close to the true value. A repeat reading catches slips. It cannot fix a method that is wrong every time: read from the same wrong angle twice and you get the same wrong number. Pressing Zero with the bowl on (taring) and taking the bowl's mass away later are both correct. Taring leaves less to go wrong. A data logger takes readings at set times, so its graph shows when a temperature has stopped changing.","In the lab your child slides a pencil until its end sits on 0. They pour to 260 ml by working out that each mark is 20 ml. They move an eye down until it is level with the water, and the reading drops from too high to 300 ml. They zero the scales with the empty bowl before adding an apple. Last, they wait for the data logger line to go flat, then take three readings that match."]},"familyTip":"Bake or make a drink together. Before each reading, ask your child what one small mark on the jug is worth. Put the jug on the table and crouch down so your eyes are level with the water. Press Zero on the kitchen scales with the empty bowl on before each new ingredient.","keyIdea":"Measuring accurately means getting a number you can trust, with its standard unit (cm, ml, g, °C). Start a ruler at 0, not at its end. Before you read a scale, work out what one small mark is worth: count the gaps between two numbers, not the marks. Read a measuring jug with your eye level with the water. Press Zero on the scales with the empty bowl on. Wait until a thermometer stops changing, then read it again to check.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Careful or careless? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Careful","❌ Careless"],"items":[["Put the end of the pencil on 0",0],["Line the pencil up with the end of the ruler",1],["Crouch so your eye is level with the water",0],["Read the jug looking down from above",1],["Press Zero with the empty bowl on",0],["Read the thermometer the moment it goes in",1],["Read it again to check",0],["Count each small mark as 1 without checking",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagscale","caption":"Flagship lab: line a pencil up with 0, pour to a mark, move your eye level with the water, zero the scales, and watch a data logger until the readings match.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ZFwPZaDJ0_":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How do you show 5 with tally marks?","choices":["4 marks and 1 across them","5 marks in a row","Write the number 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a little gate: 4 posts and a bar across.","explain":"Draw 4 marks, then the fifth goes across them. Gates of 5 are quick to count."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A tally has one gate and {r} more marks. How many is that?","answer":"5+r","hint":"The gate is 5. Count on the extra marks.","explain":"The gate is 5. 5 and {r} more is {=5+r}.","params":{"r":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You measure a sunflower every week. Which record is best?","choices":["A table with a row for each week","A tally chart","One drawing at the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"You are writing down measurements, one for each week.","explain":"A table keeps each week's height in its own row, so you can compare the weeks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You find a woodlouse. How can you record what it looks like?","choices":["Draw it and label its parts","Make a tally","Write one number"],"answer":0,"hint":"There is one woodlouse. Are you counting, measuring or showing?","explain":"There is nothing to count. A labelled drawing shows its legs, body and feelers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why should you write data down as you go?","choices":["So you do not forget and can answer the question","To fill up the page","It does not matter when you write it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens if you try to remember 12 birds?","explain":"Things happen fast and memory slips. A record made at the time is the evidence you use to answer the question."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The bean was 5 cm on day 2. Where do you write it?","choices":["In the day 2 row","In the day 1 row","Anywhere on the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each day has its own row in the table.","explain":"Write 5 cm in the day 2 row. If it goes in the wrong row, the days get mixed up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Tally: cats {c}, dogs {=c+k}. How many more dogs than cats?","answer":"k","hint":"Count on from the cats to the dogs.","explain":"From {c} to {=c+k} is {k}, so there are {k} more dogs.","params":{"c":[2,6],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Tally: cat 3, dog 6, fish 2. The fish was counted last. Which pet came the most?","choices":["Dog","Fish, because it came last","Cat, because it came first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the counts, not the order.","explain":"Dog has 6, the biggest count. The order things came in does not change the count."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You want to know how many children walk to school and how many come by car. Which record is best?","choices":["A tally chart","A drawing of a car","Just remember"],"answer":0,"hint":"You are counting, and the children arrive one at a time.","explain":"A tally chart lets you make one mark for each child as they arrive. Then you count the marks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A tally shows {g} gates of 5 and {r} more marks. How many is that?","answer":"5*g+r","hint":"Each gate is 5. Count in fives, then add the extra marks.","explain":"{g} gates of 5 make {=5*g}. Add {r} more marks to get {=5*g+r}.","params":{"g":[1,3],"r":[0,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A table says the bean was {a} cm on day 1 and {=a+d} cm on day 3. How many cm did it grow?","answer":"d","hint":"Find the difference: count on from {a} to {=a+d}.","explain":"From {a} cm to {=a+d} cm is {d} cm, so the bean grew {d} cm.","params":{"a":[2,6],"d":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Tally: robin 5, sparrow 4, pigeon 3. Which bird came the most?","choices":["Robin","Pigeon, the biggest bird","Sparrow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the row with the most marks.","explain":"Robin has 5 marks, the most. The size of a bird does not change the count."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What makes a science drawing useful?","choices":["Labels with lines that point to the parts","Lots of bright colours","Making it as big as the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how someone else would know which part is which.","explain":"Labels name each part, and label lines show exactly where it is."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you find something out, write it down straight away. Then you will not forget it. This is called recording data. Data is what you count, measure or see.","Pick the right record. To count things, like birds, use a tally chart. Make one mark for each bird. The fifth mark goes across the other four. That makes a gate of 5, so you can count in fives.","To keep numbers you measure, use a table. A bean plant has a row for each day. Write its height in the right row. Then you can see how much it grew.","To show what a thing looks like, draw it. Add labels with lines that point to each part, like shell, feelers and foot. Last, look at your record to answer your question. Do not guess."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. The KS1 working scientifically strand asks children to gather and record data to help answer questions, and to observe closely using simple equipment. Key words: data, record, observe, measure, tally chart, tally mark, table, row, column, labelled diagram, label line, and question.","Common mistakes: trusting memory instead of writing data down as it happens; drawing five tally marks in a row instead of four with a fifth across, or counting a gate as 4 or as 1; writing a reading in the wrong row of a table; drawing a pretty picture with no labels; and answering the question from a hunch, such as the biggest bird or the one seen last, not from the record.","Choosing the record is part of the science. A tally suits counting events as they happen, a table suits measurements over time, and a labelled drawing suits describing what something looks like. Asking 'which record will answer our question?' links recording back to the enquiry.","In the lab your child matches three questions to a tally chart, a table or a labelled drawing. They tally 12 birds as they land at a feeder, read a bean's height on a ruler for three days and write it in a table, and label a snail's shell, feelers and foot. Last, they use the finished tally chart to say which bird came most and least."]},"familyTip":"Sit by a window for five minutes with a pencil and paper. Ask: which comes past more, cars or bikes? Make a tally for each one. Then count the gates of 5 to answer. Another day, plant a bean in a cup, stand a ruler next to it and write its height in a table each morning.","keyIdea":"Scientists write down what they find as they go, so they do not forget and can answer their question. Pick the record that fits the question. To count things as they happen, use a tally chart: one mark for each, with the fifth mark across the first four to make a gate of 5. To keep measurements, like a plant's height each day, use a table with one row for each day. To show what something looks like, make a drawing and label its parts with lines. Then use the record, not a guess, to answer the question.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which record would you use? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Tally chart: count it","Table: measure it","Drawing: show it"],"items":[["How many cars go past?",0],["Which fruit do children pick?",0],["How tall is my sunflower each week?",1],["How warm is the water each hour?",1],["What does this leaf look like?",2],["What parts does a ladybird have?",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrecord","caption":"Flagship lab: pick a record, tally birds, fill in a bean's height table, label a snail and answer from the tally.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nNYo5A-7Bl":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a weakness of a design?","choices":["Something it does badly","Something it does well","The colour of it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A strength is something it does well. A weakness is the opposite.","explain":"A weakness is something a design does badly, like a boat that is slow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Boat A holds 12 marbles but is slow. Boat B is fast but holds 7. You must carry 10 marbles. Which boat?","choices":["Boat A","Boat B","Either one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Boat B holds 7. Can it carry 10 without sinking?","explain":"Only Boat A can hold 10 marbles. Boat B would sink, even though it is faster."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A plane flew 5 steps, then 6 steps, then 1 step. Which result does not fit?","choices":["1 step","5 steps","6 steps"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the result far from the others.","explain":"1 step is far from 5 and 6. Something went wrong in that throw, so test again to check it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ana wants her boat to go faster. She adds a sail and makes it bigger and paints it, all at once. What is the problem?","choices":["She cannot tell which change helped","Boats cannot have sails","Nothing, that is the best way"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many things did she change?","explain":"Change one thing at a time, then test. If you change three things at once, you cannot tell which one helped."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Tower A stood {a} blocks tall before it fell. Tower B stood {b} blocks tall. How many more blocks did Tower A have?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the gap between the two towers.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}. Tower A was {=a-b} blocks taller.","params":{"a":[12,18],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why do we test both designs in the same way?","choices":["So only the design is different","So the test is quicker","So our favourite wins"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what else could change the result.","explain":"If everything else is the same, any difference in the results comes from the design. That is a fair test."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Boat A held {a}, {a} and {b} marbles in 3 tests. How many marbles did it hold in all?","answer":"a+a+b","hint":"Add the three results together.","explain":"{a} add {a} add {b} is {=a+a+b} marbles in all.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is a strength of a raincoat design?","choices":["It keeps you dry","It rips easily","It is too heavy to wear"],"answer":0,"hint":"A strength helps the coat do its job.","explain":"Keeping you dry is what a raincoat is for, so that is a strength. Ripping and being heavy are weaknesses."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Sam likes the tall cup boat best, but the wide tray held more marbles. How should he choose a boat for a heavy load?","choices":["Use the test results","Pick the one he likes","Pick the tallest one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the test results, not at which boat you like best.","explain":"Use the test results. The wide tray held more, so it is the better choice for a heavy load."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Two boats are tested. Boat A holds 12 marbles. Boat B holds 7. Which boat is stronger?","choices":["Boat A","Boat B","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number is bigger: 12 or 7?","explain":"Boat A held 12 marbles and Boat B held 7, so Boat A can carry more weight."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Boat A held {a} marbles. Boat B held {b} marbles. How many more did Boat A hold?","answer":"a-b","hint":"How many more means find the gap: take the smaller number away from the bigger one.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}. Boat A held {=a-b} more marbles than Boat B.","params":{"a":[11,15],"b":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mo tests his paper plane once. It falls straight down. What should he do next?","choices":["Throw it a few more times the same way","Say it is a bad plane","Paint it a new colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can one try go wrong? What would show what the plane usually does?","explain":"One test can go wrong. Testing a few more times shows what the plane usually does."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A tower of cups stood for {a} seconds. A tower of blocks stood {b} seconds longer. How long did the block tower stand?","answer":"a+b","hint":"The block tower lasted longer, so add the extra seconds.","explain":"{a} add {b} is {=a+b} seconds. The block tower stood for {=a+b} seconds.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"To test which umbrella keeps a teddy drier, what should be the same for both umbrellas?","choices":["The amount of water poured on them","The colour of each umbrella","Who gets to hold them"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could make one teddy wetter even if the umbrellas were the same?","explain":"Pour the same amount of water on each. Then only the umbrella design is different, so the test is fair."}],"explain":{"kid":["A design is a plan for something that solves a problem. Two people can make two different designs for the same job, like two boats to carry marbles.","To find out which works better, test both. Do each test the same way, so only the design is different. That is a fair test. Test more than once. One try can go wrong. If one result is far from the others, test again to check it.","Look at what the tests show. A strength is a thing a design does well. A weakness is a thing it does badly. Most designs have both.","Pick the design that fits the job. Then make it better. Change one thing, test it again and see if it helped."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 8. This is NGSS K-2-ETS1-3: analyse data from tests of two objects built to solve the same problem, and compare the strengths and weaknesses of each. In England, Key Stage 1 science asks children to do simple tests, gather and record data, and use what they see to suggest answers. Key words: design, problem, test, fair test, data, result, compare, strength, weakness, improve.","Common mistakes: picking the design they like, or the one that looks bigger, not the one the data backs. Trusting a single test. Changing more than one thing between tests, so they cannot tell what made the difference. Thinking one design must win at everything. Adding the two results when the question asks how many more.","Keep the measures concrete and countable at this age: marbles held, seconds to cross, number of throws that landed in a hoop. Help your child put the results in a simple table or bar chart before deciding.","In the lab your child loads two foil boats until each sinks, fixes an unfair test, repeats it three times and spots the odd result, races the boats to find each one's strength and weakness, then chooses a boat for the job and improves it with one change."]},"familyTip":"Fold two paper aeroplanes: one with wide wings and one with thin, pointy wings. Throw each one 3 times from the same spot and mark where it lands with a sock. Which flew further most of the time? Which flew straighter? Ask your child which plane they would pick for a long-distance contest, and one change they would try next.","keyIdea":"When two designs try to solve the same problem, test them both in the same way and write down what happens. Repeat each test a few times, because one try can go wrong. Then use the results to say what each design does well (a strength) and what it does badly (a weakness). Choose the design that fits the job best, and make it better by changing one thing and testing again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help make a test fair? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Keep it the same","Does not matter for this test"],"items":[["How much water is in the tub",0],["The size of the marbles",0],["Putting marbles in one at a time",0],["The colour of the boat",1],["The name you give the boat",1],["What day you do the test",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdesign","caption":"Flagship lab: load two foil boats until they sink, make the test fair, repeat it, race the boats, then choose one for the job and improve it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_X0Tr8IYaEd":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Children with bigger feet tend to read better. Why?","choices":["Older children have bigger feet and read better","Big feet help you read","Reading makes your feet grow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a hidden third thing that causes both?","explain":"Age causes both. Older children have bigger feet and have had more years of reading."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Big fires have lots of fire engines. Which is true?","choices":["A big fire makes them send more engines","More engines make the fire bigger","Fire engines and fires are not linked"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which comes first, the big fire or the engines?","explain":"The fire comes first and its size decides how many engines are sent. The arrow points from fire to engines."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Tom scored 2 goals the first time he wore new boots. What can he say?","choices":["It could be luck: test many games with and without","The boots make him score","The boots make him worse"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is one game enough to tell?","explain":"One game is too few. He needs lots of games in each pair of boots before comparing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"With lucky socks Mia won {w} of 10 games. Without them she won {w} of 10. How many more wins did the socks give her?","answer":"w-w","hint":"Take the wins without socks from the wins with socks.","explain":"{w} − {w} = 0. The socks made no difference, so they do not cause wins.","params":{"w":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Towns with more shops also have more dogs. What is the best explanation?","choices":["Bigger towns have more people, so more of both","Shops make people buy dogs","Dogs make shops open"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could cause both shops and dogs?","explain":"Town size is the hidden cause. More people means more shops and more dogs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word means 'two things go up and down together'?","choices":["correlation","cause","fair test"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the pattern, not the reason.","explain":"A correlation is the pattern of two things changing together. It does not say which, if either, is the cause."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam tests a toy car on a ramp. He changes the height of the ramp and keeps the car, the ramp and the floor the same. If he tests {h} heights 3 times each, how many runs is that?","answer":"3*h","hint":"Repeating each height makes the test more reliable.","explain":"{h} heights × 3 runs = {=3*h} runs. Repeating each one helps show the pattern is not luck.","params":{"h":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Children who eat breakfast tend to do better at school. What is the best thing to say?","choices":["Breakfast might help, but something else could cause both","Breakfast is sure to make you do better","Doing well at school makes you eat breakfast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Going together is not proof. Could a third thing cause both?","explain":"Homes with time for breakfast may also help in other ways, like early bedtimes. Only a fair test could show breakfast itself is the cause."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On sunny days a beach sells more ice creams and more people get sunburn. What causes the sunburn?","choices":["the strong sun","eating ice cream","the ice cream van"],"answer":0,"hint":"Close the van in your head. Would people still burn?","explain":"The sun causes both. Ice creams and sunburn only go together because sunny days bring more of each."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Lots of open umbrellas go with rain. Which causes which?","choices":["Rain makes people open umbrellas","Umbrellas make it rain","Neither: it is luck"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask which one happens first, the rain or the umbrellas.","explain":"The rain comes first and people open umbrellas because of it. The cause runs from rain to umbrellas."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"On a hot day the beach had {s} sunburns. The next hot day the ice cream van stayed shut, and there were {s} sunburns again. How many sunburns did shutting the van stop?","answer":"s-s","hint":"Compare the two numbers of sunburns.","explain":"{s} before and {s} after means 0 were stopped. Shutting the van changed nothing, so ice cream was not the cause.","params":{"s":[4,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"To test her lucky socks fairly, Mia plays {n} games wearing them and the same number without. How many games does she play in all?","answer":"2*n","hint":"She needs one group with socks and an equal group without.","explain":"{n} with socks and {n} without makes {=2*n} games. Only then can she compare the wins.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is a fair test of whether light helps cress grow?","choices":["Two trays the same, one in light and one in a dark cupboard","One tray on a sunny windowsill","A tray in light watered more than a tray in the dark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change one thing only and keep a second tray to compare.","explain":"Two matching trays with only the light changed is fair. One tray has nothing to compare with, and changing water too mixes up two causes."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things go together. On hot days, a beach sells lots of ice creams. Lots of people get sunburn too. When one goes up, so does the other. Scientists call this a correlation.","But ice cream does not cause sunburn. The hot sun causes both. A hidden cause like this can make two things go together. So going together does not prove that one thing causes the other.","Sometimes one thing does cause the other, but the other way round. Rain makes people open umbrellas. Umbrellas do not make it rain. Ask which thing happens first.","And sometimes it is just luck. Two wins in lucky socks is too few to tell. To find a cause, do a fair test. Change one thing. Keep the rest the same. Try it lots of times."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 10. The National Curriculum in England asks pupils in Years 3 to 6 to use results to draw simple conclusions and, by Year 6, to identify scientific evidence that has been used to support or refute ideas. NGSS for grades 3 to 5 lists Patterns and Cause and effect as crosscutting concepts: a pattern can be evidence, but cause and effect has to be tested. Correlation means two measures change together; causation means changing one makes the other change.","The lesson teaches three reasons a correlation may not be causal. A confounder (a third variable) drives both, as the sun drives ice creams and sunburn and age drives foot size and reading. Reverse causation runs the arrow backwards, as with umbrellas and rain. Chance makes small samples look meaningful, as with two wins in lucky socks. The remedy is a fair test that changes one variable, controls the others and repeats the trial.","Common mistakes: reading any pattern as proof ('ice cream gives you sunburn'); spotting a link but getting the direction wrong; trusting one or two results; and calling a test fair when it has nothing to compare with, such as watering one plant and never leaving a second plant dry. Children also over-correct and say a pattern is 'just luck' when the cause is clear and comes first, so the lab includes pairs where one thing really does cause the other."]},"familyTip":"Spot 'go-together' claims at home: 'I always lose my keys when I'm late' or 'our team wins when Gran watches'. Ask three questions: could something else cause both, could it be the other way round, and have we tried it enough times? Then plan a fair test you could really do.","keyIdea":"Two things that go up and down together have a correlation. That does not prove one causes the other. A hidden third thing can cause both (hot sun brings more ice creams and more sunburn), the cause can run the other way (rain makes people open umbrellas, not the reverse), or the pattern can be luck from too few tries (two wins in lucky socks). To show a cause, run a fair test: change one thing, keep everything else the same, repeat it, and see whether the result changes.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Was the cause shown by a fair test, or are the two things only seen together? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["fair test showed it","only seen together"],"items":[["Same seeds and soil: only the plant in the light grew",0],["Kids with bigger feet read better",1],["Same ball, dropped from higher: it bounced higher each time",0],["Busy beaches sell more ice creams and have more sunburn",1],["Hats and freckles: people with sun hats have more freckles",1],["Same paper plane, bigger wings: it flew further in 5 throws",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtogeth","caption":"Flagship lab: reveal a beach week, close the van or hide the sun, pick which way the cause runs, test lucky socks, and sort claims.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_obF-6VYRya":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Mia thinks heavy balls fall faster. She drops a heavy ball and a light ball together, and they land at the same time. What should Mia do?","choices":["Change her idea","Drop them again until the heavy ball wins","Say the result is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the result fit her idea?","explain":"The balls landed together, so the result does not fit her idea. Mia should change her mind."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam tests {n} things in water. {s} of them sink. How many float?","answer":"n-s","hint":"Each thing either sinks or floats. Take the sinkers away from all of them.","explain":"{n} take away {s} is {=n-s}, so {=n-s} things float.","params":{"n":[6,10],"s":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A result surprises you. Is that bad?","choices":["No: a surprise shows where your idea needs to change","Yes: it means you failed","Yes: you should test until it fits"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a surprise tell you about your idea?","explain":"A surprise is useful. It shows you where your idea is wrong, so you can make it better."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these is good science?","choices":["I changed my mind because of what I saw","I hid the result that did not fit","Everyone thinks it, so it must be true"],"answer":0,"hint":"Good science follows the evidence.","explain":"Changing your mind because of evidence is good science. Hiding results or trusting a crowd is not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Idea: plants need soil to grow. Cress grows on wet cotton wool. What does this show?","choices":["The idea does not fit: some plants grow with no soil","Cress is not a real plant","The idea fits: cotton wool is like soil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was there any soil in the cotton wool? Did the cress still grow?","explain":"There was no soil, but the cress grew. The idea does not fit, so change it: plants need water and light."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Your idea fits {a} results. Then you find {b} more that do not fit. How many results do you have now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the results that fit and the ones that do not.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} results. Even one that does not fit means the idea must change.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your new idea fits every result so far. Can it still change?","choices":["Yes, if new evidence does not fit","No, it is proved for ever","No, ideas never change"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens when you find a result the idea cannot explain?","explain":"An idea that fits all the results so far is good, but new evidence can still change it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Big rock sinks. Small coin sinks. Big apple floats. Does 'big things sink' fit?","choices":["No: the apple and the coin do not fit","Yes: the rock sank","Yes: most things sank"],"answer":0,"hint":"An idea has to fit every result, not just one or most.","explain":"The big apple floated and the small coin sank, so the idea does not fit all the results."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"You test {n} things. {k} of the results fit your idea. How many results do NOT fit?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start with all the results, then take away the ones that fit.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Those {=n-k} results do not fit, so your idea needs to change.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ana's idea is: small things float. She tests {n} small things, and {s} of them sink. How many results do NOT fit her idea?","answer":"s","hint":"Her idea says small things float. Which results went the other way?","explain":"Each small thing that sank went against her idea, so {s} results do not fit. The {=n-s} that floated fit.","params":{"n":[5,8],"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Your idea is: big things sink. A big apple floats. What should you do?","choices":["Change the idea, because the apple does not fit","Keep the idea, because apples do not count","Hide the apple result"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the big apple do what the idea says?","explain":"The idea says big things sink, but the big apple floated. The result does not fit, so the idea must change."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You test 6 times. 5 results fit your idea and 1 surprises you. What should you do first?","choices":["Test again to check the surprise","Forget the surprise","Throw the idea away straight away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could the odd result be a slip?","explain":"One odd result could be a slip. Test again. If the surprise happens again, change the idea."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which new idea fits all of these: a big rock sinks, a small coin sinks, a big apple floats?","choices":["What it is made of matters, not how big it is","Big things float and small things sink","Round things float"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check each idea against every result. The rock is big and sank.","explain":"The rock and coin are stone and metal, and they sank. The apple floated. Size does not explain it, but what it is made of does."}],"explain":{"kid":["An idea is a guess about how the world works. Big things sink is an idea. To find out if it is right, you test it. Then you look at what happens.","Look at every result. A big rock sinks. That fits the idea. A big apple floats. That does not fit. One result that does not fit means the idea is not right yet.","A surprise is good news. It shows you where your idea is wrong. But one odd result could be a slip. Test it again. If it happens again, change your mind.","Then make a better idea that fits all the results. Wood and cork float. Stone and metal sink. Test the new idea too. It can still change if you find something new."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 8. The National Curriculum in England asks Key Stage 1 pupils to use their observations to suggest answers to questions, and lower Key Stage 2 pupils to use results to draw simple conclusions and suggest new questions and predictions. NGSS K-2 science practices ask children to use evidence to support or revise claims. This lesson is about the habit itself: compare each result with the idea, and revise when it does not fit.","The science used: whether a solid object floats depends on what it is made of compared with water (its density), not on its size. Wood, cork and apples float; solid stone and metal sink. Hollow shapes such as a metal boat can float, which a later lesson explains. Dropped together from a short height, a heavy and a light ball of the same size land at about the same time. Cress and many plant cuttings grow with no soil, because what plants need is water, light and air.","Common mistakes: children keep an idea because most results fit, explain away a surprise ('that one doesn't count'), test again and again until they get the answer they wanted, or flip the idea to its opposite without checking it against every result. The opposite slip is changing an idea after one odd result without testing again. The lab stops each of these with a named reason."]},"familyTip":"Fill a bowl with water. Before you test, say an idea out loud, such as 'big things sink'. Try a big apple, a small coin, a wooden spoon and a metal key. After each one, ask: did that fit our idea? When something surprises you, cheer, test it again, and then make a better idea together.","keyIdea":"A scientist tests an idea and looks at every result. If a result does not fit the idea, the idea has to change. One surprising result could be a slip, so test again to check; if it happens again, change your mind. A surprise teaches you more than a result you expected. A good new idea fits all the results so far, and it can still change if new evidence does not fit.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Idea: big things sink, small things float. Does each result fit the idea? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["fits the idea","does not fit"],"items":[["a big stone sank",0],["a big log floated",1],["a small coin sank",1],["a small cork floated",0],["a big apple floated",1],["a small pebble sank",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrethink","caption":"Flagship lab: test 'big things sink' in a water tank, decide what a scientist does next, test a better idea, and sort plant evidence and good science.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
